{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.GrowingWidthCutShapeExclusion","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.GrowingWidthCutShapeExclusion","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Sqrt","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.measured_constant_error_at_least_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.measured_constant_error_at_least_two","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GrowingWidthCutShapeExclusion.lean#L110-L128","doc":"**Instantiated at the measured spread.**  With `rmin = 0.5130070` (²H) and\n`rmax = 2.3770636` (⁴He) — the rescalings the balanced cut `w = 3/2` would need\n— every constant is off by at least a factor of two somewhere.  The probe's\ndense value is 2.1526×; two is the floor this bound certifies.\n\nThe two literals are measured inputs, not Lean-derived. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.best_constant_error_lower_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.best_constant_error_lower_bound","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GrowingWidthCutShapeExclusion.lean#L94-L108","doc":"**The quantitative form.**  If the per-isotope rescalings that would be\nrequired span `[rmin, rmax]`, then for EVERY positive constant `c` the worst\nmultiplicative error is at least `√(rmax / rmin)`.\n\n`rmax / c` is how far the constant undershoots the isotope needing the most;\n`c / rmin` how far it overshoots the one needing the least.  No `c` escapes\nboth. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.sqrt_mul_le_max","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.sqrt_mul_le_max","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GrowingWidthCutShapeExclusion.lean#L85-L92","doc":"The geometric mean never exceeds the max. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.mono_hypothesis_is_load_bearing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.mono_hypothesis_is_load_bearing","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GrowingWidthCutShapeExclusion.lean#L76-L83","doc":"**Non-vacuity II.**  The monotonicity hypothesis is load-bearing, not\ndecorative: a model that DOES decrease across the two points matches the same\ndecreasing data exactly.  This is why the probe must verify `hg` for every `w`\nrather than assume it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.matching_possible_when_data_nondecreasing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.matching_possible_when_data_nondecreasing","kind":"theorem","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GrowingWidthCutShapeExclusion.lean#L69-L74","doc":"**Non-vacuity I.**  The conclusion is not forced by the shape of the\nhypotheses: drop only `hb` (let the data be non-decreasing too) and matching\nbecomes possible.  So the theorem really is about the ⁴He peak. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.no_constant_scale_matches_of_mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.no_constant_scale_matches_of_mono","kind":"theorem","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GrowingWidthCutShapeExclusion.lean#L56-L67","doc":"**The exclusion.**  A model that does not decrease across two points cannot,\nafter any non-negative rescaling, reproduce data that does decrease across them.\n\n`g₁, g₂` are the model's per-nucleon values at ⁴He and ⁶Li; `b₁, b₂` the AME\nones; `c` an arbitrary overall scale.  The hypothesis `hg : g₁ ≤ g₂` is what the\nprobe verifies for every `w`, and `hb : b₂ < b₁` is the ⁴He peak. ","signatureChunk":0}]}